         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"unitaryCircle\" id=\"circtri0\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 170px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 340\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 320 147 A 30 30 0 0 1 340.0 170.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"470\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"8\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"169\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"170\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 170)\"></circle> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"448\" y2=\"169\"></line> <line class=\"radius-45\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"66\"></line>  <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"406\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"406\" y2=\"166\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"188\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"88\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"360\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">a</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"390\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"320\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"282\" y=\"187\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"407\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"405\" y=\"182\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">Q</text></svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Circle with a radius of 1 containing a right triangle.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Trigonometry (Sine)\n\nIn the second text on the Pythagorean Theorem (<htcite0>), we explored how to draw a right triangle and saw that, from the center **O**, we could reach any point **P** on the circle by traveling along the legs or along the hypotenuse.\n\nThen, in the cosine text, we connected the angle theta (θ) to the adjacent leg (<htcite1>) — one of the triangle's two legs. In this text, we connect the angle to the other leg. Together, these texts lead us to a reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "c9870ac9-4722-489a-a51c-52576dd5f2c6",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "The Theorem and the Circle",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c95f9bf-befa-42bd-8d56-0cf962e09c14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Cosine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: What about the opposite leg?</b><br /><br />The right triangle has two legs. How does the opposite leg relate to the angle theta (θ)?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Sine\n\nThe detailed study of the relationships between the angle and the triangle's elements is known as trigonometry (<htcite0>). Through trigonometry, we study functions such as the sine, defined in **Formula 1** (<htcite1>). There, the *opposite leg* is the leg that does not touch the angle theta (θ) — leg **b** in our figures:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0098b5fe-1cb7-4451-82c9-8e5d5d4f20e6",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Trigonometry",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eb59db91-042f-4acb-8211-47eac4348c94",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Sine",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>opposite leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac></math></p></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Definition of sine.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Let's see whether the sine function describes the opposite leg just as effectively. We will follow the same order as the cosine analysis (<htcite0>) to make comparison easy.\n\n#### The Maximum Value\n\nTo keep things simple, we will isolate from **Graph 1** the parts that matter for each step.\n\nStart with the radius **c** lying exactly on the vertical line: leg **b** then has the same length as the radius (**Graph 2**):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "4c95f9bf-befa-42bd-8d56-0cf962e09c14",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Trigonometry (Cosine)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 170\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 170 A 168 168 0 0 0 200 22\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 200 140 A 30 30 0 0 1 230.0 168.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"168\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"168\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"169\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"23\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"169\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"200\" y=\"19\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"205\" y=\"77\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"208\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 2</b>: The hypotenuse lying on the opposite leg: the angle theta (θ) is 90°.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Since leg **b** has the same length as the radius **c** in **Graph 2**, that is, **b = c**, and the angle theta (θ) is 90° (a right angle between the vertical radius and the horizontal), the sine reaches its greatest possible value: 1, as shown in **Calculation 1** (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "112e532d-e8af-40d6-b1b5-527978ee42f3",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Two sides",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>opposite leg</mi><mi>hypotenuse</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Maximum value obtained for the sine of theta (θ).</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Intermediate Values\n\nAs the radius leaves the horizontal line, the angle theta (θ) moves away from zero, as shown in **Graph 3**.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <path class=\"radius-30\" d=\"M 230 130 A 30 30 0 0 1 240.0 150.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"149\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"319\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"67\"></line> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"319\" y2=\"67\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"250\" y=\"89\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"325\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text>  <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"300\" y=\"116\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">b</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"223\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"1.0em\" font-weight=\"bold\">θ</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 3</b>: The angle theta (θ) opening between the hypotenuse and the horizontal line.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Another consequence of this rotation is that leg **b** gets longer. This is no coincidence: leg **b** is the projection of the hypotenuse **c** onto the vertical axis — its \"shadow\" on that line.\n\nBoth behaviors we have just described affect the sine of theta (θ) directly. As leg **b** (the projection) grows, the sine grows with it, because we are dividing a larger leg by the same constant: the hypotenuse.\n\n#### The Minimum Value\n\nThe smallest value we can see in the graphs on this page occurs when leg **b** has length zero. That happens whenever the radius (our hypotenuse) is completely horizontal, as shown in **Graph 4**:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 160\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\">  <defs> <marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"3\" markerHeight=\"3\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"> <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" /></marker> </defs> <path d=\"M 348 150 A 148 148 0 0 0 200 2\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#71a6d2\" stroke-width=\"3\"/> <line x1=\"200\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"black\" stroke-width=\"3\" style=\"stroke: black;\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\" /> <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"200\" y1=\"149\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"149\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"280\" y=\"138\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">c</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"180\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"350\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">P</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 4</b>: The radius lying completely horizontal, so the opposite leg has length zero.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusions\n\nThe sine of theta (θ) captures exactly the projection of leg **b** as the radius rotates around the center **O**. Formula 1 already contains this fact; multiplying both sides by the hypotenuse (the radius **c**) makes it explicit:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><mn>(c)</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>b (1)</mn></math></p> <p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>(c) sin (θ)</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>b</mn></math></p> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Projection of the hypotenuse onto the horizontal axis.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "From **Calculation 2**, we can confirm that:\n- **Line 1**: Here leg **b** grows from the horizontal until it reaches the greatest length possible inside the circle: the radius **c**. At that point the sine equals 1. In the other direction, as leg **b** shrinks back toward the horizontal, the sine falls until it reaches zero, when the radius lies completely horizontal.\n- **Line 2**: The radius **c** acts as a scale factor on both sides of the calculation. To see how the leg depends on the radius and on the angle, we add that factor to both sides — that is, we multiply both sides by **c**.\n- **Line 3**: On the right side we were already dividing by the radius, so multiplying by it again leaves **c/c**, which equals 1. That is why the right side becomes **b (1)**.\n- **Line 4**: Finally, this line says that as the sine gets smaller, leg **b** gets shorter: the angle is decreasing, so the projection **b** shrinks.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Practicing\n\nNow it is time to practice what we have just learned. In **Graph 2**, the radius stood on the vertical line: there, leg **b** had the same length as the radius **c**, and the sine of theta (θ) reached its greatest value: 1. The interactive figure below starts in exactly this position. As you move the radius toward the horizontal line, the angle theta (θ) shrinks, leg **b** shortens, and the sine falls; move the radius back up and the sine returns to 1.\n\nClick on the chart to move the radius inside the first quadrant. Watch the right triangle, the angle theta (θ), and the value of the sine change. Move the radius to different positions and try to anticipate the value of the sine before reading it in the panel below the chart. For example, when the angle is 30°, the sine is exactly 0.5 — see if you can find that position.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"cscPracticeInstruction\" style=\"text-align:center;\">Click on the chart to move the radius in the first quadrant. Observe how the opposite leg (and the sine) decrease as the radius approaches the horizontal line.</p> <div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><div id=\"senpracticedc29\"></div></div> <p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 5</b>: Interactive practice: moving the radius in the first quadrant, the sine decreases as the radius approaches the horizontal line.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
